we've got labour's shadow work and  pension secretary johnson reynolds  on the programme i'm not sure how much  of that you managed to catch but we just  saw a film  showing the impact of school closures on  young people and many struggling to get  the technology  they need anxiety worry about not  getting the grades they  deserve at what point do you think  schools need to reopen  it was an incredibly powerful film i did  just  hear it and credit to those young people  for speaking to you so candidly  the way that they did that is why we  wanted to keep schools open  as long as possible unfortunately the  department of education didn't take the  steps  necessary to keep them open for longer  but you know to hear young people  describe how they still haven't got  access to technology haven't got access  to  broadband at this stage of the pandemic  is inexcusable clearly to answer your  question directly schools can only go  back  when the health factors are such that  they allow them to go back but there is  no excuse  for the government not acting to make  sure people have technology  and access to online learning at home  none whatsoever it's difficult isn't it  because  looking forward you say that schools can  only go back when the health  factors allow them to go back my  question i guess is  what does that actually mean i mean  labor you know you're in a position  where  you have been criticized i guess for  calling for things that are going to  happen anyway you know this is a chance  to kind of set out a road map  at what point do you think schools  should be able to go back  what's your roadmap for that to happen  it will depend on the rollout of the  vaccination program it will depend on  the infection rates in each area and it  will depend on the state or the nhs and  whether it's on the brink of being  overwhelmed as it is  in many parts of the country you know  there are things  that the government can't control about  the pandemic but there is lots that they  can control  and the response from government to  situations like this but also to what  we're talking about  on monday in poland in terms of  universal credit and the support given  to people  who have to be at home who can't work  these are things the government can do  you can't get away from the fact that we  have suffered in the uk  the highest death toll in europe and  the biggest recession of any major  economy  that comes down to how the government  has responded to it so they've got to be  scrutinized for that  and how we get schools back will depend  on how they take decisions now  like the one we're asking them to take  tomorrow you mentioned that the decision  on  universal credit i know something you're  keen to talk about on your brief of  course so in april the 20 pound a week  boost to  universal credit is due to end rishi  soon at the chancellor wants to give  families a 500 pound one-off payment  instead  is that enough no i mean i'm glad you've  raised that because that is quite  frankly  a terrible policy first of all if you go  ahead with this cut  you are reducing out of work support  unemployment benefits  to their lowest real terms level since  1992  at a time when unemployment is about to  peak  but of course the reason a one-off  payment is a bad policy is  whilst we're talking about six million  families being affected those families  will change throughout the years and  we'll go  back into works and we'll come out we've  had at times of the pandemic  two hundred 000 new claimants coming  onto the system in a single month so a  one-off payment a snapshot  completely fails to support those people  there is simply  no reason that this cut should take  place in april  it's the wrong thing for families i mean  a thousand pounds a year  is like your gas your electric your  broadband bill all rolled in together  but it's also the wrong thing for the  economy that is six billion pounds of  spending  being taken out of the economy at a time  when we need it the most frankly so the  priority for us  is to secure the economy protect the nhs  and then rebuild the country and to do  that  parliament has to do the right thing  tomorrow and conservative mps of course  due to abstain on that and opposition  database non-binding  i guess my question as well to you is  labour's position  as i understand it is that universal  credit should be scrapped  in the middle of a pandemic is that the  right priority still when we talk about  scrapping universal credit  that's about our priorities for the next  parliament and the election manifesto  that we will stand on  there are huge problems with universal  credit it's not just labour mps who  recognize that  that people see the five-week weight the  impact of the two-child limit  the sanctions the conditionality the the  treatment of disabled people  in the system the treatment of child  care which is always  has to be paid up front yet universal  credit is paid in arrears  i can't ignore those real problems so i  have to come up with a system  much more comprehensive much more  effective than universal credit as it  stands and that's exactly what i  intend to do but for now for what needs  to happen  this week and this year there is a vote  tomorrow in parliament to cancel  that cut of a thousand pounds a year for  six million  british families there is no question  that parliament should do the right  thing  and vote to cancel that could are you  talking to conservatives  about it are you expecting a rebellion  there i can't predict what conservative  colleagues  will do but yes people have been in  touch there's been some productive  conversations look when was the last  government  to cut out of work benefits in  a major economic downturn i think you  have to go back to the great depression  and the national government to find that  there is a reason this never happens  there is a reason it is not in the  national interest it is absolutely  the wrong priority 